In this use case, we consider eNodeB-level energy-consumption estimation as a downstream regression task. Given the RAN profiles of the sectors associated with an eNodeB, the goal is to predict its measured energy consumption using the pretrained foundation model. This task provides a downstream target that differs from the KPI prediction tasks considered previously and allows us to evaluate whether the pretrained model can be adapted to energy-related network measurements.

Since the foundation model operates at the sector level while energy consumption is measured at the eNodeB level, we first obtain the representation of each sector and concatenate the representations of sectors belonging to the same eNodeB. The resulting eNodeB-level representation is passed to a regression head to predict energy consumption. During fine-tuning, the regression head and the pretrained model are jointly optimized end-to-end. We use [DATA DESCRIPTION], yielding [XX] eNodeB-level samples, which are split into training, validation, and test sets using [SPLITTING STRATEGY].
